# The question and the answer so far

Can private memory be removed from a deterministic online BST algorithm
at constant-factor cost? This remains unresolved. Two results are proved:

\begin{leanbox}
\textbf{Purely additive simulation is impossible.} A fixed two-state
algorithm on three keys defeats every tree-only algorithm by an unbounded
additive amount.\\[5pt]
\textbf{Constant-factor state elimination is equivalent to tree-only dynamic
optimality in this model.} Allowing an arbitrary sequence-independent additive
function instead of $O(n)$ does not change this existence question.
\end{leanbox}

Both results are checked in Lean, without \code{sorry} or extra axioms.
The equivalence does not establish either existence statement.

# What an algorithm can remember

A tree shape determines a BST on the inorder ranks $0,\ldots,n-1$.
An access to $x$ is a sequence of rotations ending with $x$ at the root.
Equivalently, the final root has a left subtree of size $x$.
The following definition adds explicit private state to the original model:

\leansnippet{StatefulAlgorithm}

A step receives state, tree, and request, and returns new state, tree, and
rotation count. Correctness certifies the access. Initialization sees only
the initial tree. All algorithms here are deterministic.

\leansnippet{TreeAlgorithm}

\code{Unit} has one value. A tree-only rule can inspect the entire current
shape, but cannot retain a clock, access history, or other private information.
It may still encode information by changing that shape.

We charge one unit per request plus one per rotation:
\[
 \cost_A(T,X)=|X|+\text{rotations used by }A.
\]
Planning computation is uncharged. Rotations may occur anywhere in the tree;
pointer travel is not charged. The results below concern this precise model.
A cost-preserving connection to a standard pointer model remains to be proved.

\newpage
# A three-key obstruction to additive simulation

There are five three-key shapes. Their single-rotation graph is a pentagon.
In the figure, an edge represents one rotation in either direction.

: figure[ht]
  \centering
  \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.94]
    \foreach \name/\x/\y in {a/-2.8/0,b/-1.7/-2.1,d/1.7/-2.1,e/2.8/0,c/0/1.55}
      \coordinate (\name) at (\x,\y);
    \foreach \u/\v in {a/b,b/d,d/e,e/c,c/a}
      \draw[edge,line width=1pt] (\u)--(\v);
    \foreach \name in {a,b,c,d,e}
      \node[fill=white,draw=figblue,rounded corners,minimum width=17mm,minimum height=17mm] at (\name) {};
    \begin{scope}[shift={(a)},scale=0.48,key/.append style={minimum size=3.5mm,inner sep=0pt,font=\scriptsize}]
      \node[key] (a0) at (-.5,.8) {0}; \node[key] (a1) at (0,0) {1}; \node[key] (a2) at (.5,-.8) {2};
      \draw[edge] (a0)--(a1)--(a2);
    \end{scope}
    \begin{scope}[shift={(b)},scale=0.48,key/.append style={minimum size=3.5mm,inner sep=0pt,font=\scriptsize}]
      \node[key] (b0) at (-.5,.8) {0}; \node[key] (b2) at (.5,0) {2}; \node[key] (b1) at (-.5,-.8) {1};
      \draw[edge] (b0)--(b2)--(b1);
    \end{scope}
    \begin{scope}[shift={(d)},scale=0.48,key/.append style={minimum size=3.5mm,inner sep=0pt,font=\scriptsize}]
      \node[key] (d2) at (.5,.8) {2}; \node[key] (d0) at (-.5,0) {0}; \node[key] (d1) at (.5,-.8) {1};
      \draw[edge] (d2)--(d0)--(d1);
    \end{scope}
    \begin{scope}[shift={(e)},scale=0.48,key/.append style={minimum size=3.5mm,inner sep=0pt,font=\scriptsize}]
      \node[key] (e2) at (.5,.8) {2}; \node[key] (e1) at (0,0) {1}; \node[key] (e0) at (-.5,-.8) {0};
      \draw[edge] (e2)--(e1)--(e0);
    \end{scope}
    \begin{scope}[shift={(c)},scale=0.48,key/.append style={minimum size=3.5mm,inner sep=0pt,font=\scriptsize}]
      \node[target] (c1) at (0,.6) {1}; \node[key] (c0) at (-.8,-.6) {0}; \node[key] (c2) at (.8,-.6) {2};
      \draw[edge] (c1)--(c0); \draw[edge] (c1)--(c2);
    \end{scope}
    \node[above=9mm,font=\small] at (c) {$c$: the only shape rooted at $1$};
    \node[left=9mm] at (a) {$a$}; \node[right=9mm] at (e) {$e$};
    \node[below=9mm] at (b) {$b$}; \node[below=9mm] at (d) {$d$};
  \end{tikzpicture}
  \caption: Every access to $1$ forces the shape back to $c$, erasing all
    information that a tree-only algorithm might have encoded in its shape.

Start at $c$ and repeat the eight-request word
\[
 W=(0,2,1)\,(0,2,0,2,1).
\]
A one-bit algorithm distinguishes these two blocks. It uses the
following endpoint paths; a jump across two pentagon edges costs two rotations:
\[
 c\longrightarrow a\longrightarrow e\longrightarrow c
 \quad\text{and}\quad
 c\longrightarrow b\longrightarrow d\longrightarrow b\longrightarrow d\longrightarrow c.
\]
These paths cost $4+7=11$ rotations. Including eight requests, the cost is $19$.
The bit flips after each planned access; shape and request identify the
remaining phase. Unexpected requests use a legal fallback, so this is an
online algorithm on all inputs, not merely a serving schedule for one word.

A tree-only algorithm faces the same pair $(c,0)$ at the start of both blocks.
It must make the same choice there. A finite case analysis proves that its two
blocks together require at least $12$ rotations, hence total cost at least $20$.
Both blocks end at $c$, so the argument repeats indefinitely:
\[
 \cost_A(c,W^m)=19m,\qquad \cost_B(c,W^m)\ge20m.
\]
For any fixed $f(3)$, choosing $m>f(3)$ refutes
$\cost_B\le\cost_A+f(n)$. More generally, every rational coefficient below
$20/19$ fails for this same $A$. This does not rule out a larger constant factor.
The lower bound is in \code{ThreeKeySeparation.lean}; the one-bit algorithm is
in \code{OneBitSeparation.lean}.

\newpage
# Why arbitrary additive slack does not make the question easier

The constant-factor question has quantifiers
\[
 \forall A\;\exists B,C_A,f_A\;\forall T,X:\quad
 \cost_B(T,X)\le C_A\cost_A(T,X)+f_A(|T|).
\]
The constants may depend on $A$. They cannot depend on tree size or input
length. For a fixed $n$, the additive term is one constant for every sequence.
The same initial tree is used on both sides.

Call a tree-only algorithm ***universally competitive*** if
\[
 \exists C,D\;\forall T,X:\quad
 \cost_B(T,X)\le C\OPT(T,X)+D|T|,
\]
where $\OPT$ is the minimum request-plus-rotation cost of any offline execution
from $T$. In Lean we quantify over every execution rather than define $\OPT$.

\leansnippet{Equivalence}

The second theorem says that the additive allowance can be restricted to
linear without changing the existence question. It does not claim that the
same simulator produced by an arbitrary state-elimination argument already
has a linear allowance.

\textbf{Easy direction.}
A universally competitive tree-only algorithm competes with every online
algorithm, since each online run is also a legal offline execution.

\textbf{Hard direction: one probing algorithm suffices.}
Construct one stateful learner that eventually predicts every ultimately
periodic request sequence. It keeps the least still-consistent candidate
(prefix, period). On an actually ultimately periodic input, all smaller false
candidates are eventually eliminated, so its chosen forecast stabilizes.
For each forecast, a fixed good infinite serving plan is chosen. Resets align
the actual tree with that plan; after stabilization no further resets are needed.
The checked construction uses classical choice and unbounded history.

This learner matches the average cost of every periodic offline schedule,
up to an additive constant depending on the particular prefix and period.
If state elimination holds, one tree-only $B$ must simulate this single learner.

Now follow the pair of current trees: $B$'s tree and an offline execution's
tree. An edge has weight
\[
   \text{cost of }B\text{'s access}-C\,\text{cost of the offline access}.
\]
A reachable positive cycle could be repeated forever. The learner would
asymptotically match the offline loop, while $B$ would accumulate positive
excess each repetition, contradicting any additive allowance independent of
sequence length. Thus there are no positive cycles.

For fixed $n$, this graph is finite. Erasing nonpositive cycles bounds the
excess of every path by a function of $n$. A final reset-and-potential argument
reduces that allowance to $O(n)$. This proves the displayed equivalence,
without producing a universally competitive tree-only algorithm.

\newpage
# What the reset idea proves, and what it still needs

The proposed argument has a useful ingredient: two trees of size $n$ can be
converted into one another in at most $2n$ rotations. The checked construction
passes through a canonical right spine. Performing a reset \emph{before} the
next access preserves the requirement that each access finish at its requested
root.

But equal tree shape does not imply equal private state. Selecting a good
starting state for one observed block does not ensure it is good for every
possible continuation. Nor does an $O(n)$ reset restore that private state.
Repeated blocks in the three-key example demonstrate a real additive loss;
for constant-factor simulation, the missing uniform choice remains unresolved.

There is nevertheless a checked, stronger use of resets. Suppose a tree-only
algorithm already satisfies
\[
 \cost_B(T,X)\le C\OPT(T,X)+f(n).
\]
For two starting trees $s,t$, define the least nonnegative credit needed to
compare $B$ from $s$ with offline executions from $t$ over all continuations.
Changing $t$ changes this credit by at most $2Cn$. For each size there is a
pair of trees requiring zero credit; otherwise a worst-case continuation
could repeatedly spend positive credit and contradict boundedness.

Using one such pair as a reference, the new tree-only rule either follows
$B$, or resets to the reference tree when the credit of its present shape
is too large. The trigger depends only on that shape and fixed data for $n$.
There is no phase counter. A potential pays for the resets and proves
\[
 \boxed{\cost_{B'}(T,X)\le C\OPT(T,X)+(2+6C)n.}
\]
The coefficient $C$ is unchanged. This is \code{normalize\_additive} in
\code{AdditiveNormalization.lean}. The construction is noncomputable and
assumes offline competitiveness to begin with; it is not a direct simulation
of an arbitrary stateful algorithm.

# Finite tests and the remaining target

Every positive comparison cycle has a witness with a short reaching prefix
and a short repeating block. Lean now bounds both lengths by a finite
size-dependent cover of tree pairs. Consequently only finitely many
(prefix, period) request words need to be tested at each $n$.

This suggests a probe with finite memory for each $n$: eliminate candidates
from that finite list and follow precomputed minimum-average serving cycles.
The bounded-witness and bounded-probe reduction are checked. The finite-memory
construction itself is currently a mathematical argument, not a Lean theorem.
Its memory bound would grow with $n$; it says nothing about a fixed number of
private states independent of $n$.

\textbf{The remaining question is precise:} construct one tree-only algorithm
with a uniform constant competitive factor, or prove that none exists in
this rotation model. The reset lemmas, periodic probe, and cycle arguments
reduce and clarify this task. They do not yet solve it.

